1:
Paste this into the editor and press Run.

```ts
const { browser, page } = await launch();
await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

await page.getByPlaceholder("First Name").fill("Grace");
await page.getByPlaceholder("Last Name").fill("Hopper");
await page.locator("textarea").fill("1 Compiler Lane");
await page.locator("input[type='email']").fill("grace.hopper@example.com");
await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill("5559876543");

await show(page);
await browser.close();
```

**Why each locator is the one it is.** First Name and Last Name both carry unique placeholder text, so `getByPlaceholder` reads best. Address has **no** placeholder and no label — but it is the only `<textarea>` on the page, so `page.locator("textarea")` is unambiguous. Email and Phone are reached by input type: `input[type='email']` and `input[type='tel']` each match exactly one control here.

**Check it the way the problem asks:** the screenshot shows all five filled, and no line threw. If one had matched nothing, Playwright would have waited the full timeout and then failed with a clear "waiting for locator" — not silently skipped it.

> `.fill()` and not `.type()` — Day 1.2 measured 68 ms against 92 ms on the same string. Reach for `.pressSequentially()` only when the page reacts to individual keystrokes, such as an autocomplete.

2:
```ts
const { browser, page } = await launch();
await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

// The label on this page really is spelled "FeMale" - a gotcha, not a typo to fix.
await page.locator("input[value='FeMale']").check();

await page.locator("#checkbox3").check(); // Hockey
await page.locator("#checkbox1").check(); // Cricket - a second hobby, allowed

// #Skills looks like a multi-select (78 options) but has no `multiple` attribute,
// so it keeps only the LAST pick. Two calls, one survivor.
await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption({ label: "Java" });
await page.locator("#Skills").selectOption({ label: "Python" });

// Two selects mention Country. #countries ("Country*") is a dead field with only a
// placeholder option; the live one is #country, labeled "Select Country :".
await page.locator("#country").selectOption("India");

await show(page);
await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
await browser.close();
```

**The two traps this problem is built around.**

**"Two skills" is not achievable on this control.** `#Skills` has no `multiple` attribute, so the second `selectOption` replaces the first rather than adding to it — the screenshot will show Python alone. That is the page being honest about what it is. Recognizing it beats writing a passing test that claims two skills were chosen.

**`FeMale`.** Searching the DOM for `Female` returns nothing, and it is tempting to assume the radio is broken. Read the attribute before you trust your memory of it.

> Take the screenshot **before** the click. After submit the page changes, and you lose the evidence of what you actually filled in.

3:
Two edits. First `week2/generated/registration-helpers.ts` — add a second parameter rather than a second function:

```ts
import type { Page } from "@playwright/test";

export async function fillRegistrationForm(page: Page, email: string, phone: string) {
  await page.goto("https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html");

  await page.getByPlaceholder("First Name").fill("Ada");
  await page.getByPlaceholder("Last Name").fill("Lovelace");
  await page.locator("textarea").fill("123 Analytical Engine Way");
  await page.locator("input[type='email']").fill(email);
  await page.locator("input[type='tel']").fill(phone);
}
```

Then a spec that calls it twice:

```ts
import { test } from "@playwright/test";
import { fillRegistrationForm } from "./registration-helpers";

test("registers learner one", async ({ page }) => {
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.one@example.com", "5550000001");
  await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
});

test("registers learner two", async ({ page }) => {
  await fillRegistrationForm(page, "learner.two@example.com", "5550000002");
  await page.locator("#submitbtn").click();
});
```

```bash
npx playwright test week2/generated/day1-practice-reuse.spec.ts
```
→ `2 passed`.

**The judgement being trained here.** The obvious alternative is a second function, `fillRegistrationFormWithPhone`. Resist it: two functions that differ by one line drift apart the first time the form changes, and then you have two truths about the same page. A parameter keeps one.

The values that *stay* hard-coded, the name and the address, stay that way deliberately. Parameterize what a caller actually varies; parameterizing everything just reinvents `page.fill` with extra steps.

> The helper file is `registration-helpers.ts`, **not** `.spec.ts`. Give it the spec suffix and the runner will collect a file containing no tests.
